From 876d8c7cff4ccca83eeb97160ebb70dd6214c4c6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason <jdg511@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 07:44:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add 12 component-modelled clipper / dynamics circuits +
 radial chooser

Replaces the four ad-hoc waveshapers (Clean/Silicon/LED/Germanium) with
twelve selectable circuits whose behaviour is derived from real parts.

Nine clippers are solved from the Shockley diode equation against the
actual component values, not hand-tuned curves:

  Silicon Sym/Asym  1N4148 across Rf (TS808 / SD-1)
  Schottky Sym      BAT41, lowest headroom in the set
  LED Sym           red LED, clips latest and stays open
  LED/Si Asym       widest asymmetry here, 1.85 V against 0.72 V
  SiGe Asym         PMEG150G20ELP, early clip with a hard knee
  Transistor B-E    2N3904 junctions, n = 1.0 so a harder knee
  Ladder Sym/Asym   3+3 and 3+2 series stacks shunting to ground

The SiGe part is fitted to the Nexperia PMEG150G20ELP datasheet: Is and n
come from the 25 C curve of Fig. 3 in the uA-mA region a clipper works in,
not the amp-level table rows, which carry ~58 mohm of series resistance
that is irrelevant at signal currents. Is = 2.863e-10 A, n = 1.134, giving
Vf 0.44 V @ 1 mA. Worth noting n = 1.13 against the 1N4148's 1.75 makes it
sharper-kneed than silicon, not softer - it is not a germanium substitute.

The two topologies behave differently on purpose: feedback clippers keep
rising with unity slope past the knee so they stay touch sensitive, while
the shunt ladders hard-clamp at the stack's forward voltage. Op-amp rail
saturation at +/-4.5 V is part of the model, so heavy Drive gives real
supply clipping on top of the diodes.

Three one-knob dynamics circuits model an LA-2A (Vactrol, dual-slope
release), an 1176LN (2N5457 FET, 150 us attack, 4:1, FET grit included)
and a dbx 160 (THAT2180 VCA, true-RMS detector, over-easy knee). Like the
hardware they have no threshold control - Drive pushes them into a fixed
threshold and make-up keeps the level steady.

Curves are solved once per process into warped lookup tables (88 ms, off
the audio thread) and shared by every instance; nothing allocates or
iterates in processBlock. Every circuit is RMS level matched so moving
through them changes character, not volume.

UI: the Mode combo box becomes a detented rotary selector reading out the
circuit name, with a description strip beneath the control row.

models/clipper_models.py is the Python reference for the same equations;
tests/ verifies monotonicity, level matching and dynamics behaviour and
is kept out of the plugin build.

Note: the mode parameter went from 4 choices to 13, so sessions saved
with the old build will restore a different circuit.

